; The four states the battle log names -- SkillEffectProcessLeaderState@Evaluate
; writes "%s: %s！" and fills the second field from this function, so this is
; where the log gets "Rance: Stunned！".
;
; Three of the four could be, and were, translated as strings. The fourth cannot:
; s[3897] = "ダウン" is also what BattleInsertionProcessor@InnerProcess runs
; String.Contains against, over tokens split out of the enemy AI conditions --
; those are data and still Japanese, so a translated slot stops that test from
; ever matching. The rule the repository keeps for exactly this case is to leave
; the string alone and patch the function that displays it, which is what
; patches/card_names.jaf does for card names and build/race_names.jaf for races.
;
; Which means all four move here, not just the one. A literal in a .jam is
; assembled against the string table as it stands when alice-tools reads the
; file, and the text patch has already run: S_PUSH "スタン" would match no slot
; -- s[3965] says "Stunned" by then -- and come back as a new Japanese string
; that this function would push. The enemy panel spent a long while drawing
; 種族：%s for that reason. So the English is written in directly, and
; patches/system_cherry_picks.v1.04.ain.txt keeps s[3965] through s[3967] only as
; a comment pointing here. "Unknown" is the exception that needs no thought: it
; is s[174], a slot shared far too widely to be anything but a text patch, and
; it already reads English by the time this file is assembled.
;
; getStateNameFromSkillType returns the same three names from a second switch and
; is left as it is -- it has no callers, the way 表示規模 has none.
;
; Save this file as UTF-8 with no BOM. The assembler reads a byte order mark as
; an instruction and stops on line 1 -- "Invalid instruction: ϻ".
;
; The body is that function verbatim from
;
;   alice ain dump -c game/ain/Rance10.v1.04.ain
;
; with the five strings swapped and nothing else touched; regenerate it the same
; way if the game version changes, since every number in it indexes one build of
; one .ain. It adds no local, which is what makes it possible at all -- --jam
; rewrites code and leaves the FUNC section alone, so a function keeps the
; locals it was compiled with.
FUNC 27472
	.LOCALREF proc
	PUSH 27334
	CALLMETHOD 0
	SWITCH 434
	JUMP 0x4368a6
.CASE 434:0 2
	S_PUSH "Stunned"
	RETURN
.CASE 434:1 4
	S_PUSH "Downed"
	RETURN
.CASE 434:2 3
	S_PUSH "Petrified"
	RETURN
.CASE 434:3 0
	S_PUSH "Boosted"
	RETURN
0x4368a6:
	S_PUSH "Unknown"
	RETURN
	S_PUSH ""
	RETURN
ENDFUNC SkillEffectProcessLeaderState@GetStateName
